{
  "uid": "kuat-hosts",
  "title": "kuat / Hosts",
  "description": "CPU, memory and temperature for corelia and meksha. Both hosts are on one dashboard on purpose: if one side is empty and the other is full, the monitoring is fine and that host is off. If both are empty, the monitoring is broken. Metrics prefixed arakyd_ come from the arakyd collector; the rest from node_exporter.",
  "tags": [
    "kuat",
    "hosts"
  ],
  "timezone": "browser",
  "schemaVersion": 39,
  "version": 1,
  "refresh": "1m",
  "time": {
    "from": "now-6h",
    "to": "now"
  },
  "editable": true,
  "panels": [
    {
      "id": 100,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "Availability \u2014 read this first",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "Host reachable",
      "type": "stat",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 1,
        "w": 8,
        "h": 5
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "max by (instance) (up{job=\"hosts\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "mappings": [
            {
              "type": "value",
              "options": {
                "0": {
                  "text": "DOWN",
                  "color": "red",
                  "index": 0
                },
                "1": {
                  "text": "UP",
                  "color": "green",
                  "index": 1
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "special",
              "options": {
                "match": "null",
                "result": {
                  "text": "NEVER SCRAPED",
                  "color": "purple",
                  "index": 2
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "1 = the scrape succeeded. 0 = it failed, which is the host being off OR node_exporter not running \u2014 those are indistinguishable from here. No data at all means the scrape job itself is gone, which is a monitoring failure rather than a host one. Wrapped in max by (instance) so one row appears per host however many series carry that instance. Two things produce more: a label change on the scrape job leaves the old label set inside Mimir's 5-minute lookback, so an instant query briefly returns both (the role -> host_role rename did exactly that, and showed meksha as UP and DOWN at once); and Alloy is a DaemonSet, so a second node would scrape these targets twice, permanently. max is the right reduction for 'is it reachable' \u2014 if anything reached it, it is up.",
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "colorMode": "background",
        "graphMode": "none",
        "textMode": "value_and_name"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "title": "Last seen",
      "type": "stat",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 8,
        "y": 1,
        "w": 8,
        "h": 5
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "time() - max by (instance) (last_over_time(timestamp(up{job=\"hosts\"} == 1)[7d:1m]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "s"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "Seconds since the last successful scrape. timestamp() is applied INSIDE and carried by a subquery: timestamp() of an already-filtered instant vector returns the evaluation time, not the sample's, which reads as 0 forever.",
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "none",
        "textMode": "value_and_name"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "title": "arakyd collector",
      "type": "stat",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 16,
        "y": 1,
        "w": 8,
        "h": 5
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "max by (instance) (arakyd_collector_success + 2 * (timestamp(arakyd_collector_last_run_seconds) - arakyd_collector_last_run_seconds > bool 90))",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "mappings": [
            {
              "type": "value",
              "options": {
                "0": {
                  "text": "FAILING",
                  "color": "red",
                  "index": 0
                },
                "1": {
                  "text": "OK",
                  "color": "green",
                  "index": 1
                },
                "2": {
                  "text": "STOPPED",
                  "color": "red",
                  "index": 3
                },
                "3": {
                  "text": "STOPPED",
                  "color": "red",
                  "index": 4
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "special",
              "options": {
                "match": "null",
                "result": {
                  "text": "n/a",
                  "color": "text",
                  "index": 2
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "node_exporter being up does not mean arakyd ran. FAILING: the collector ran and read nothing (no temperature, or a GPU whose readings are gone). STOPPED: the file node_exporter serves is over 90 s old at scrape (the timer runs every 30 s), so nothing is rewriting it - a disabled timer, a removed script, a unit failing before it starts - while success still reads 1 in it; success alone cannot show this (Kamino item 131). Wrapped in max by (instance) so one row appears per host however many series carry that instance. Two things produce more: a label change on the scrape job leaves the old label set inside Mimir's 5-minute lookback, so an instant query briefly returns both (the role -> host_role rename did exactly that, and showed meksha as UP and DOWN at once); and Alloy is a DaemonSet, so a second node would scrape these targets twice, permanently. max is the right reduction for 'is it reachable' \u2014 if anything reached it, it is up.",
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "colorMode": "background",
        "graphMode": "none",
        "textMode": "value_and_name"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 101,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "CPU",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "title": "CPU busy",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 7,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 7
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "100 - (avg by (instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{job=\"hosts\",mode=\"idle\"}[5m])) * 100)",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percent",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100
        },
        "overrides": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "title": "Load average (1m) per core",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 12,
        "y": 7,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 7
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "node_load1{job=\"hosts\"} / on(instance) group_left count by (instance) (node_cpu_seconds_total{job=\"hosts\",mode=\"idle\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {},
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "Normalised by core count, so 1.0 means saturated regardless of the machine. corelia has 32 threads, meksha 8 \u2014 the raw figures are not comparable."
    },
    {
      "id": 102,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "Memory \u2014 including what the iGPU holds",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 14,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 20,
      "title": "Memory available",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 15,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 7
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes{job=\"hosts\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} available",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "expr": "node_memory_MemTotal_bytes{job=\"hosts\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} total",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "MemAvailable, not MemTotal minus used: most of a loaded model's footprint is reclaimable page cache, so 'used' says very little. Readings 20 minutes apart on corelia have legitimately shown 35, 1 and 72 GiB."
    },
    {
      "id": 21,
      "title": "iGPU memory held (GTT)",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 12,
        "y": 15,
        "w": 12,
        "h": 7
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "arakyd_gpu_memory_bytes{pool=\"gtt\",state=\"used\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} GTT used",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "expr": "arakyd_gpu_memory_bytes{pool=\"gtt\",state=\"total\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} GTT ceiling",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "Strix Halo has no meaningful dedicated VRAM: the iGPU allocates out of system RAM through GTT. node_exporter has no concept of this, so without arakyd this memory is simply invisible. It is the number that decides whether a model loads."
    },
    {
      "id": 103,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "Temperature and power",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 22,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 1
      },
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 30,
      "title": "Temperature by role",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 23,
        "w": 16,
        "h": 8
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "arakyd_temperature_celsius{role=~\"cpu|igpu|nvme\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} {{role}} ({{sensor}})",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          },
          "expr": "node_hwmon_temp_celsius{job=\"hosts\",instance!=\"corelia\",chip=~\"platform_coretemp.*|nvme.*\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}} {{chip}} ({{sensor}})"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "celsius"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "Two sources, because only corelia runs arakyd. Its series are labelled by role (cpu, igpu, nvme) because arakyd applies that mapping at collection. Other hosts fall back to node_exporter's own hwmon collector, keyed by chip \u2014 platform_coretemp_0, nvme_nvme0 \u2014 hence the different legend shape. corelia is EXCLUDED from that second query rather than the query being pinned to one host: corelia's nvme appears in both, so without the exclusion its disk temperature is drawn twice."
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "title": "APU package power (PPT)",
      "type": "timeseries",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "mimir"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 16,
        "y": 23,
        "w": 8,
        "h": 8
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "arakyd_gpu_power_watts",
          "legendFormat": "{{instance}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "mimir"
          }
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "watt"
        },
        "overrides": []
      }
    }
  ]
}
